Day 3: Viewing & Editing Files
Commands Practiced
echo "text" > file — write text into a file, overwriting existing content
echo "text" >> file — append text to a file without erasing it
cat <file> — print the entire file content (best for short files)
less <file> — page through long files (Space to scroll, q to quit)
head <file> / head -N <file> — show first 10 (or N) lines
tail <file> / tail -N <file> — show last 10 (or N) lines
tail -f <file> — live-follow a file as new lines get added (used constantly to watch live logs during deployments)
vim <file> — terminal text editor
i → insert mode (start typing)
Esc → back to normal mode
:wq → save and quit
:q! → quit without saving (emergency exit)
Key Takeaways
> vs >> is a critical distinction — using > by mistake wipes a file’s existing content instantly.
cat floods the screen on large files; less and head/tail are the practical tools for real log files.
tail -f is one of the most-used commands in real DevOps work — watching an application log live while deploying or debugging.
